# Minimal Diff for Code Review

When refactoring (locking, rebucketing, API migration), optimize for **reviewer signal**, not local brace aesthetics.

## Rules

1. **Preserve control-flow shape** — keep `do { ... } while (0)`, early `break`, existing brace style.
2. **Do not re-indent unchanged bodies** — when adding an outer lock/`for`/guard, only change wrapper lines; inner statements stay at their original column.
3. **Prefer `XPON_VANI_CONFIG_GUARD`** over explicit `lock`/`unlock` for short sections; minimal-diff is about **avoiding body re-indent**, not avoiding the guard macro.
4. **No cosmetic helper extraction** — do not move an unchanged loop into a static function solely to make the parent look smaller.
5. **Extract helpers only when justified** — e.g. snapshot under lock then release before slow I/O, or real duplication removal.

## Wrapper pattern (C)

```c
    int _pon;
    for (_pon = 0; _pon < NUM_BUCKETS && cond; _pon++)
    {
        LOCK_GUARD(_pon);
        STAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE(hdr, &list[_pon], next, hdr_tmp)
    {   /* body indentation unchanged vs pre-refactor */
        /* ... original lines untouched ... */
    }
    }
```

Replace `LOCK_GUARD` / list name only at the wrapper; the foreach body should produce **zero diff lines**.

## Self-check

```bash
git diff <base> -- path/to/file.c
```

If unchanged logic appears as modified lines, fix wrapping/indentation before opening CR.
